    confidence: 'LOW',
  },
];

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Where is Port Khalid / Sharjah Port?',
    a: 'Port Khalid is the Sharjah port in the United Arab Emirates. It is listed among the six UAE ports — Jebel Ali, Khalifa, Zayed, Fujairah, Rashid and Sharjah — named in the China DocShipper freight guide, and is the natural port enquiry for Sharjah-bound freight.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the UN/LOCODE for Port Khalid?',
    a: 'The UN/LOCODE is AESHJ, defined in the WorldFreightHub GCC ports data as Sharjah / Port Khalid. Use it on the bill of lading and customs paperwork when the destination is Sharjah.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Who operates Port Khalid?',
    a: 'The verified snapshot does not publish the current port authority or terminal operator for Port Khalid. Confirm the operator, berth allocation and fee schedule directly with the Sharjah port authority or your forwarder before relying on any terminal-specific detail.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does sea freight take from China to Sharjah?',
    a: 'The verified snapshot does not publish a direct China–Sharjah transit figure. As corridor context, the China→UAE sea guide is 14–18 days, and the WorldFreightHub Jebel Ali route data is a typical 21 days with a 15–30 day range. If the cargo relays through Jebel Ali, add the relay and final trucking leg; always request the actual vessel schedule.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How does China freight actually reach Port Khalid?',
    a: 'Some services call Port Khalid directly, while others discharge at Jebel Ali and relay or truck the container to Sharjah. The verified snapshot does not specify the current sailing pattern, so ask the forwarder whether the quote is a direct Sharjah call or a Jebel Ali relay plus final leg.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I route Sharjah cargo through Port Khalid, Jebel Ali or Khalifa?',
    a: 'Match the discharge port to the consignee and final inland leg. Port Khalid is usually the better fit for Sharjah and northern-emirate consignees; Jebel Ali is the default for Dubai, re-export and transhipment; Khalifa Port Abu Dhabi suits Abu Dhabi and KIZAD. Confirm inland distance, cost and transit before choosing.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What free zones are associated with Sharjah freight?',
    a: 'Sharjah has industrial and free-zone clusters, commonly cited examples including Hamriyah Free Zone. The verified snapshot does not publish zone boundaries, licensing terms or incentives, so treat that as directional and verify the specific zone with the relevant Sharjah authority before assuming any duty or VAT benefit.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What import taxes apply at Sharjah?',
    a: 'The baseline UAE import duty is 5% of CIF value under the GCC Common External Tariff, and UAE import VAT is 5%, calculated on CIF value plus customs duty. Higher duty or excise can apply to specific goods such as tobacco, alcohol and motor vehicles.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the UAE de minimis threshold?',
    a: 'The verified snapshot records a de minimis of Dhs 1,000 duty/tax free for goods, with gifts up to Dhs 3,000 exempt. This is a courier/e-commerce planning figure; confirm the current rules with UAE Customs before relying on it for a specific shipment.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What documents are needed for clearance at Sharjah?',
    a: 'The standard set is a commercial invoice, bill of lading, packing list and certificate of origin. Classify the goods with the correct HS code before booking, because both duty and any permit requirements depend on it.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do demurrage and detention work at Port Khalid?',
    a: 'Demurrage is charged by the terminal when an imported container stays inside the port beyond free time after discharge. Detention is charged by the ocean carrier when the container stays outside the port beyond free time. Finish clearance and book trucking before discharge to avoid both.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is ISF required for a China to Sharjah shipment?',
    a: 'No. ISF is a United States import filing requirement and does not apply to UAE imports. Do not treat US customs requirements as UAE requirements.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does Sharjah freight require SABER or SASO certification?',
    a: 'No. SABER and SASO are Saudi Arabian conformity systems. They do not apply to UAE imports. For UAE goods, confirm the UAE-specific regulatory and permit position from Dubai/Federal Customs or the relevant authority.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Are Port Khalid berth, depth and throughput figures verified?',
    a: 'The verified facts are the UN/LOCODE AESHJ, Sharjah’s inclusion in the UAE port list, the 5% duty, the 5% VAT and the corridor benchmarks. Port-specific berth, depth, operator and throughput figures were not published in the research snapshot, so they are marked LOW and should be verified with the port authority.',
  },
];

const organization = {
  '@id': `${SITE_URL}/#organization`,
  name: 'WorldFreightHub',
  url: SITE_URL,
  logo: {
    '@type': 'ImageObject',
    url: `${SITE_URL}/favicon.svg`,
  },
};

const schema = [
  {
    type: 'Article',
    data: {
      headline: 'Sharjah Port (Port Khalid) — China to Sharjah Freight Guide (August 2026)',
      description:
        'Sharjah / Port Khalid intelligence for China freight: UN/LOCODE AESHJ, Sharjah vs Jebel Ali vs Khalifa routing, China transit context, LCL/FCL decision support, hidden destination charges and 5% duty/VAT compliance.',
      datePublished: '2026-08-31',
      dateModified: '2026-08-31',
      inLanguage: 'en',
      mainEntityOfPage: {
        '@type': 'WebPage',
        '@id': pageUrl,
      },
      author: {
        '@type': 'Organization',
        name: 'WorldFreightHub',
