      description:
        'Port Khalid is the Sharjah entry in the UAE port list used for China freight. It serves as the northern-emirate gateway for Sharjah-bound container and general cargo.',
    },
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sharjah Port (Port Khalid) — China to Sharjah Freight Guide"
  description="Sharjah / Port Khalid intelligence for China freight: UN/LOCODE AESHJ, Sharjah vs Jebel Ali vs Khalifa routing, China transit context, LCL/FCL decision support, hidden destination charges and 5% duty/VAT compliance."
  canonical={pageUrl}
  ogType="article"
  schema={schema}
>
  <div class="page-header">
    <div class="container">
      <Breadcrumb
        items={[
          { label: 'Middle East', href: '/middleeast/' },
          { label: 'Sharjah Port' },
        ]}
      />
      <span class="badge badge--amber">August 2026 updated</span>
      <h1>Sharjah Port (Port Khalid): China freight guide for Sharjah and the northern emirates</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A port-intelligence page for shippers routing China freight to Sharjah. This page explains
        what Port Khalid is, how it fits the wider UAE port list, how to choose between Sharjah,
        Jebel Ali and Khalifa, what the rate and transit picture looks like, and the 5% duty / 5% VAT
        clearance rules that apply at the gate.
      </p>
      <p class="small muted">
        Confidence badges separate verified statutory facts from indicative corridor benchmarks.
        Treat every LOW-confidence transit, facility and fee note as indicative, and verify with the
        Sharjah port authority or your carrier before relying on it.
      </p>

      <div class="fact-grid mt-3">
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">UAE import VAT</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% <ConfidenceBadge level="HIGH" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Baseline import duty</span>
          <span class="fact-value">5% CIF <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">UN/LOCODE</span>
          <span class="fact-value">{unlocode} <ConfidenceBadge level="MEDIUM" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">China sea transit</span>
          <span class="fact-value">14–18 days corridor <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">LCL benchmark</span>
          <span class="fact-value">AED 880–980/CBM <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
        <div class="fact">
          <span class="fact-label">Port role</span>
          <span class="fact-value">Northern gateway <ConfidenceBadge level="LOW" /></span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>1. Why Port Khalid matters for China freight</h2>
    <p>
      Port Khalid is the Sharjah entry in the UAE port landscape. When a China freight enquiry lists
      Sharjah among the UAE destinations, Port Khalid is the port question that follows: it is the
      discharge point that keeps Sharjah-bound cargo close to the emirate rather than routing every
      consignment through Dubai. That inland proximity is the whole commercial case for using it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The UAE port list from the China DocShipper guide names six ports — Jebel Ali, Khalifa, Zayed,
      Fujairah, Rashid and Sharjah. Sharjah therefore sits in the same UAE corridor as Jebel Ali and
      Khalifa, but the port-choice logic is different: Jebel Ali wins on line connectivity and
      transhipment, Khalifa wins Abu Dhabi, and Port Khalid wins the northern-emirate hinterland.
    </p>
    <p>
      The verified research snapshot does not publish Port Khalid berth, depth, operator or
      port-specific throughput figures. What it does give is the UN/LOCODE AESHJ, Sharjah’s place in
      the UAE port list, the 14–18 day China–UAE sea corridor benchmark, and the statutory 5% duty /
      5% VAT position. Everything else on this page is deliberately marked LOW rather than filled with
      plausible-looking numbers.
    </p>
    <p>
      China origin depth matters here too. Shanghai handled 55.06 million TEU in 2025 as the world’s
      #1 container port, and Ningbo-Zhoushan handled 43 million TEU in 2025 as the #3 container port.
      Those figures are HIGH-confidence context for why the China–UAE corridor has schedule depth —
      but they do not answer whether your specific carrier calls Sharjah directly or relays through
      Jebel Ali. That answer must come from the carrier schedule.
    </p>
    <SourceList sources={[...chinaPortSources, ...portSources]} title="Sources — Sharjah corridor & China port context" />
  </section>

  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>2. Port Khalid parameters</h2>
      <p>
        Use this table as an orientation, not a capacity guarantee. Port-specific berth, depth,
        operator and throughput figures were not published in the verified research snapshot, so those
        rows stay deliberately empty of invented numbers and point you to the Sharjah port authority
        for the current value.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'parameter', label: 'Parameter' },
          { key: 'value', label: 'Value' },
          { key: 'note', label: 'Planning note' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={parameterRows}
        caption="Port parameters — verify berth, depth, operator and per-port throughput with the Sharjah port authority before relying on these details."
      />
      <SourceList sources={portSources} title="Sources — Port Khalid parameters" />

      <div class="note mt-2">
        <strong>Capacity-sensitive cargo:</strong> if your consignment depends on a specific berth
        length, draft or crane capability, request the current terminal schedule and restrictions
        from the port authority or your forwarder before booking. The verified snapshot does not
        supply those numbers.
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>3. China to Sharjah rate benchmarks</h2>
    <p>
      Rate transparency is deliberately strict here. The verified snapshot gives a Dubai/Jebel Ali LCL
      benchmark and an air benchmark, but it does not publish a China–UAE FCL dollar range or a
      Sharjah-specific destination tariff. The FCL rows therefore say “request an all-in quote”
      instead of inventing a number.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'service', label: 'Service' },
