  <section class="section container">
    <h2>5. FCL vs LCL for Sharjah-bound freight</h2>
    <p>
      The FCL/LCL decision is a volume, handling and clearance decision, not just a price comparison.
      LCL consolidates at origin and deconsolidates in the UAE, which suits small or mixed shipments;
      FCL moves one sealed unit to one consignee. The table gives the planning heuristics.
    </p>

    <DataTable
      columns={[
        { key: 'factor', label: 'Factor' },
        { key: 'lcl', label: 'LCL' },
        { key: 'fcl', label: 'FCL' },
        { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
      ]}
      rows={lclVsFclRows}
      caption="Directional planning heuristics, not guaranteed quotes. Nominal container cube figures are planning approximations."
    />
    <SourceList sources={rateSources} title="Sources — FCL vs LCL decision context" />

    <div class="grid grid--2 mt-2">
      <Card title="Choose LCL when...">
        <ul>
          <li>The shipment is small, mixed-SKU or market-testing stock.</li>
          <li>You can tolerate origin consolidation and destination CFS handling.</li>
          <li>You are building an e-commerce or slow-restock flow into the northern emirates.</li>
          <li>Sharjah or Jebel Ali CFS deconsolidation is already in your forwarder’s plan.</li>
        </ul>
      </Card>
      <Card title="Choose FCL when...">
        <ul>
          <li>The volume approaches a 20GP, 40GP or 40HQ load.</li>
          <li>The goods are dense, fragile, branded or high-value.</li>
          <li>You want one sealed unit and one clearance entry.</li>
          <li>You are trying to avoid the damage and mixing risk of shared CFS handling.</li>
        </ul>
      </Card>
