  <section class="section section--alt">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>8. Post-arrival cost composition and hidden charges</h2>
      <p>
        The headline ocean rate is not the landed cost. Sharjah-bound cargo still faces terminal,
        clearance, document, trucking and potential storage charges after discharge. The verified
        snapshot quantifies only the statutory 5% duty and 5% VAT; every operational line is LOW
        confidence and should be itemised by the forwarder before booking.
      </p>

      <DataTable
        columns={[
          { key: 'item', label: 'Cost component' },
          { key: 'who', label: 'Who charges it' },
          { key: 'magnitude', label: 'Indicative magnitude' },
          { key: 'confidence', label: 'Confidence', type: 'confidence' },
        ]}
        rows={costRows}
        caption="Only VAT (5%) and baseline duty (5% CIF) are verified. LCL/air benchmarks and unquantified fees are LOW confidence because the snapshot did not publish specific amounts."
      />
      <SourceList sources={costSources} title="Sources — Sharjah destination costs & customs" />

      <h3 class="mt-3">The hidden-charge checklist</h3>
      <p>
        The most common surprise lines after discharge are <strong>THC and port charges</strong>,
        <strong>customs clearance/brokerage</strong>, <strong>electronic processing charges</strong>,
        <strong>inland trucking</strong>, and the two storage clocks described below. The verified
        snapshot does not publish dollar or AED amounts for these, so the practical discipline is not
        to accept a quote without them — request an itemised destination fee schedule from the
        forwarder and the nominated CFS.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Demurrage and detention</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Demurrage</strong> is charged by the terminal when an imported container stays inside
        the port beyond free time after discharge. <strong>Detention</strong> is charged by the ocean
        carrier when the container stays outside the port beyond free time. Free-time allowances and
        per-day rates differ by line and terminal and were not published in the verified snapshot, so
        always request the fee schedule before booking.
      </p>
      <p>
        The practical way to avoid both is to finish clearance before the vessel arrives: file the
        pre-arrival declaration through the UAE customs system, keep the HS code and documents ready,
        and book trucking before the container is discharged.
      </p>

      <h3 class="mt-3">Free-zone vs mainland treatment</h3>
      <p>
        If the consignee holds goods inside a Sharjah free zone, duty and VAT can be suspended while
        the cargo remains in the zone; when the goods later exit to the mainland, the normal duty and
        VAT treatment generally applies. Confirm the specific zone rules and the importer-of-record
        structure with the zone authority and your broker, because the snapshot does not publish
        zone-by-zone conditions.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container">
    <h2>9. Compliance at Sharjah: VAT, duty, HS codes and the rules that do not apply</h2>

    <h3>Tax and duty</h3>
    <p>
      UAE import VAT is <strong>5%</strong>, effective since 1 January 2018, and is calculated on the
      CIF value <em>plus</em> customs duty. The baseline import duty is <strong>5% of CIF</strong>
      under the GCC Common External Tariff, with higher duty or excise treatment possible on specific
      goods such as tobacco, alcohol and motor vehicles.
    </p>

    <h3>De minimis and declaration flow</h3>
    <p>
      The verified snapshot records a UAE de minimis of Dhs 1,000 duty/tax free for goods, with gifts
      up to Dhs 3,000 exempt. For commercial ocean freight, do not assume a courier de minimis applies;
      classify the goods with the correct <strong>HS code</strong>, establish the CIF value, and file
      the declaration through the UAE customs system before or upon arrival.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>Classify the goods with the correct HS code before quoting or booking.</li>
      <li>Confirm importer eligibility, permits and any restricted-commodity approval.</li>
      <li>Prepare the commercial invoice, packing list, bill of lading and certificate of origin so every line agrees.</li>
      <li>File the pre-arrival declaration through the UAE customs system used for the Sharjah port call.</li>
      <li>Pay the assessed duty and VAT, and complete any inspection, to obtain release.</li>
    </ol>

    <h3>Labelling</h3>
    <p>
      UAE imports can require product labelling, Arabic-language information or product-specific
      conformity evidence depending on the goods and regulator. The verified snapshot does not publish
      a Sharjah-specific labelling checklist, so treat labelling as a per-product compliance item and
      verify the current requirements with UAE Customs, the destination authority or your buyer before
      shipment.
    </p>

    <h3>Rules that do not apply to the UAE</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>ISF</strong> is a United States import filing requirement and does not apply to UAE
      imports. <strong>SABER/SASO</strong> are Saudi Arabian conformity systems and do not apply to
      UAE imports. Do not let US or Saudi requirements leak into a Sharjah-bound shipment; confirm the
      UAE-specific position instead.
    </p>

    <SourceList sources={complianceSources} title="Sources — UAE customs, VAT & compliance" />
  </section>

